SEED AND IDENTITY MODEL:
  Master seed: any string; the default is 8 random base32 characters. Hash it with cyrb128 and feed sfc32. Math.random is banned in src.
  Derivation is hierarchical and order-free: childSeed = hash(parentSeed + ":" + kind + ":" + index). Galaxy 3 is identical whether it is generated first or after galaxies 0 to 2. Never share one sequential RNG stream across siblings.
  IDs: u:{seed}/g{n}/s{n}/p{n}/m{n}. The URL hash mirrors the current level and ID (#/u/{seed}/g/2/s/41/p/3), so reload, deep links, and the browser back button all work.
  Two layers: GENERATED data is immutable and a pure function of (masterSeed, id). STATE (anything a later phase changes: ownership, discovery, resources spent) lives in a separate store keyed by ID, and saves will store STATE only. Phase 1 creates the empty STATE store and the ID scheme and stores nothing in it.
  Cross-machine determinism: gameplay-relevant values come only from CPU generation. GPU noise defines visuals only, and no game logic ever reads it back. ECMAScript leaves sin, cos, exp, log, and pow implementation-approximated, so keep them out of any value that drives a discrete choice, or quantize the result (for example to 1e-6) before comparing. Quantize selectable system positions to 1e-4 units.
  Generation is lazy: nothing below the current level is generated until the player goes there. Descriptors are cached in an LRU.

GENERATION LOGIC:

  UNIVERSE (L0):
    Galaxy count 6 to 12. Type weights: spiral 55, barred spiral 20, elliptical 15, irregular 10.
    Positions by Poisson-disk sampling inside a flattened ellipsoid with a minimum separation of 2.5 galaxy radii. Disk normal uniform on the sphere. Size factor 0.5 to 1.5.
    Each galaxy gets a seeded name and a palette: core tint, arm tint, nebula tints.
    Rendering: each galaxy is a low-detail 3D point cloud (about 20k points at HIGH), so rotating the camera shows real depth. Distant background galaxies are tiny sprites baked into the skybox.

  GALAXY (L1):
    Components: bulge (exponential radial falloff, old yellow-white stars), disk (exponential radial profile, Gaussian thickness that thins outward), arms (young blue-white stars, pink H-II knots, dust lanes on the inner edge of each arm), halo (sparse old stars).
    Spiral: logarithmic arms, theta = ln(r / a) / b + armIndex * 2 * PI / armCount, 2 to 4 arms, Gaussian scatter that grows with radius. A 2-arm grand-design variant may use the density-wave method instead: ellipses whose orientation rotates with radius.
    Barred: an elongated bar of old stars through the center; the arms start at the bar ends.
    Elliptical: triaxial Gaussian distribution, old red-yellow stars, no dust, no H-II.
    Irregular: 3 to 7 domain-warped clumps, blue-heavy, many H-II knots.
    Stellar classes: weighted O, B, A, F, G, K, M. The decorative set follows a realistic skew toward M and K. The selectable set is reweighted toward F, G, and K for gameplay variety. Color comes from blackbody temperature through a precomputed LUT in linear RGB.
    Selectable systems: 300 to 1200, sampled from the same distribution, excluded inside 0.08 of the galaxy radius, with a minimum separation and quantized positions.
    Rendering: one THREE.Points draw per component with a custom sprite shader: Gaussian core, faint four-point diffraction spikes on the brightest 0.5 percent only, size from magnitude with perspective attenuation and a clamp, HDR brightness so bloom catches bright cores. Dust uses larger soft sprites with multiplicative blending, drawn after the stars. Point clouds are built in a worker and uploaded as typed arrays.

  SYSTEM (L2):
    Stars: single 75 percent, binary 25 percent (the pair orbits the barycenter and planets are circumbinary). Rare variants: red giant 3 percent, white dwarf 2 percent. Main-sequence relations: L = M^3.5, R = M^0.8, temperature from the class table.
    Habitable zone: inner = sqrt(L / 1.1) AU, outer = sqrt(L / 0.53) AU. Frost line = 4.85 * sqrt(L) AU.
    Planet count by class: O and B 0 to 4, A 1 to 6, F and G 3 to 10, K 2 to 8, M 1 to 7.
    Orbits: first semi-major axis 0.2 to 0.5 * sqrt(L) AU, each next one multiplied by 1.4 to 2.1. Eccentricity 0 to 0.12 (rarely up to 0.3), inclination 0 to 4 degrees, random node, periapsis, and mean anomaly at epoch.
    Type by zone:
      HOT (a below 0.6 * HZ inner): lava, scorched, barren, rare hot giant.
      TEMPERATE (inside the HZ): terran, ocean, desert, toxic.
      COOL (HZ outer to frost line): desert, barren, ice, toxic.
      OUTER (beyond the frost line): gas giant near the line, ice giant further out, ice worlds, barren.
    Variety rule: a system with 4 or more planets shows at least 3 distinct types. Reroll with a derived seed, never with the parent stream.
    Physical values per planet: radius range per type, mass from a mass-radius relation, surface gravity, day length, axial tilt (0 to 35 degrees, rarely up to 90), albedo per type, equilibrium temperature T = 278 K * L^0.25 / sqrt(a) * (1 - A)^0.25.
    Moons: gas giant 2 to 8, ice giant 1 to 5, terran-class 0 to 2, small worlds 0 to 1. Moon types: barren, ice, or volcanic (tidal heating near giants).
    Rings: gas giant 35 percent, ice giant 25 percent.
    Belts: inner asteroid belt 50 percent, placed in the gap before the first giant; outer icy belt 30 percent.
    Motion: Kepler orbits, period in years = sqrt(a^3 / Mstar). Solve Kepler's equation with 5 Newton iterations. Time controls: pause, 1x, 10x, 100x, where 1x = 2 days per real second.
    Display scale: displayed orbit radius = k * sqrt(a); displayed planet radius = r0 * sqrt(radius in Earth radii). Enforce a gap between neighbor orbits of at least 3 times the larger displayed planet radius. Far planets keep a minimum on-screen size of 3 px through a sprite fallback.

  NAMES: seeded syllable tables, with no real star names and no franchise names. A system gets a name; stars get the system name plus a letter for binaries; planets get the system name plus a Roman numeral; moons get the planet name plus a lowercase letter.

PLANET SURFACE PIPELINE:
  Bake once, shade every frame. Render each planet's surface into cube map render targets on the GPU, sampling 3D noise at the unit direction of each texel, so there are no seams and no pole pinching.
  Packed targets: ALBEDO_HEIGHT (rgb albedo, a height), NORMAL_ROUGH (xyz normal from height by central differences on the sphere, w roughness), and EMISSIVE where the type has one. Generate mipmaps.
  Progressive bake: 256 per face first, then the tier size. Bake at most one face per frame and spend at most 8 ms per frame on baking, so interaction never stalls.
  Close-up detail: below 3 planet radii the fragment shader adds a detail normal from high-frequency 3D noise, faded in by distance.
  Animation lives in the frame shader, not in the bake: lava pulse, fire fronts, cloud drift, gas giant differential rotation.
  Noise library (shared GLSL): 3D simplex, fBm, ridged multifractal, billow, domain warping, Worley F1 and F2 with cell IDs. Per-planet seed offsets stay under 1000 in magnitude, because large offsets break float precision in GLSL.
  Color: each type has a curated palette family, and the seed jitters hue, saturation, and value inside the family bounds. Biome color comes from a small 2D LUT texture (temperature on x, moisture on y) instead of hand-written if chains.
  Lighting: GGX specular with per-texel roughness, a diffuse term with a soft terminator, wrap lighting with a blue tint for ice (fake subsurface), sun glint on water. At L3 the light direction matches the star's real position in the system.
  Atmosphere: a separate shell at 1.02 to 1.08 radii. Rayleigh plus Mie single scattering with 16 view and 8 light samples at HIGH, 8 and 4 at MEDIUM, and a Fresnel rim approximation at LOW. Per-type scattering coefficients and scale heights. Additive blending.
  Clouds: a separate sphere with its own baked cube map (domain-warped fBm), drifting faster than the surface, casting soft shadows by sampling the cloud map offset toward the light.
  Rings: 1D procedural band texture with gaps. Planet shadow on the rings and ring shadow on the planet are computed analytically (ray to sphere, ray to plane).

TYPE RECIPES:
  TERRAN: continent mask from low-frequency domain-warped fBm; relief from ridged multifractal inside the mask; sea level from an ocean fraction of 0.45 to 0.75; biomes from the LUT with temperature from latitude, altitude lapse, and star distance; polar caps where temperature is lowest; ocean color by depth. Roughness: water 0.08, land 0.8, ice 0.35. Clouds 35 to 65 percent. Blue Rayleigh atmosphere.
  OCEAN: ocean fraction 0.85 to 0.97, scattered archipelagos, a shallow reef tint around islands, heavy clouds, strong sun glint.
  DESERT: dunes from anisotropic ridged noise stretched along a seeded wind direction; mesas from terraced height with smoothed steps; ochre to rust palette; dusty tan Mie-heavy atmosphere; clouds 0 to 15 percent.
  BARREN: multi-scale crater function from Worley cells (bowl, raised rim, ejecta rays on the largest fresh craters); dark maria from low-frequency noise; no atmosphere; roughness 0.9.
  LAVA (REF-LAVA): crust from ridged fBm in near-black basalt and ash gray; fissure mask from Worley F2 minus F1 below a noise-modulated width, plus ridged-noise channels; magma pools in lowlands below a lava level; emissive from a blackbody color between 1400 K and 2200 K at HDR intensity 4 to 12, with a short glow falloff onto adjacent crust; a slow emissive pulse in the frame shader; a thin red-brown sulfur haze.
  ICE (REF-ICE): plates from Worley cells with small per-cell height offsets; crumpled micro-relief from high-frequency ridged noise; dark blue crevasses along cell edges; palette deep navy to cyan-white; roughness 0.15 to 0.3 varied per plate, so the specular sheen breaks up across plates; wrap lighting with a blue tint; a thin pale cyan atmosphere.
  TOXIC: thick opaque atmosphere; the surface mostly hidden under domain-warped cloud bands in yellow-green and ochre; heavy Mie haze.
  SCORCHED (REF-BURNING): terran generation with land recolored to amber and charcoal; fire fronts as animated emissive bands along a migrating fBm isoline on land; ember sparkle from thresholded high-frequency noise; dark brown smoke clouds; an orange atmospheric halo.
  GAS GIANT: no height field. Bands from sin(latitude * bandCount + fBm(latitude) * k) with turbulent domain warp that grows near band edges; 1 to 3 storm vortices made by rotating the lookup around a center by an angle that decays with distance; palettes of beige, cream, rust, and brown, or a dark maroon hot giant with a faint emissive night side; differential rotation by latitude in the frame shader.
  ICE GIANT: the gas giant pipeline with muted bands, a cyan to teal to blue palette, and a faint haze.

STAR RENDERING:
  Surface: realtime shader with no bake. Animated granulation from Worley F1 at two scales plus fBm, sunspots on G, K, and M classes, limb darkening I = 1 - 0.6 * (1 - mu), HDR emissive 20 to 50 so bloom carries it.
  Corona: a camera-facing billboard with inverse-square falloff plus slowly rotating noise streamers.
  L0 and L1 draw stars as point sprites. L2 draws the full star shader. L3 draws the star as a bright disk with glare at its true direction in the sky.

BACKGROUND:
  Bake a skybox cube map per location: hashed direction cells for stars with power-law magnitudes, and nebula from domain-warped fBm in two or three tints from the galaxy palette. At L2 and L3 add the host galaxy band aligned with the real galaxy plane: intensity = exp(-(dot(dir, galaxyNormal))^2 / w).

RENDER PIPELINE:
  HDR: render into a HalfFloat target with 4x MSAA (WebGL2 multisample render target), bloom by UnrealBloomPass with a threshold near 1.0 so only HDR emitters glow, ACES Filmic tone mapping, then a dither in the final pass to remove banding in dark gradients. Device pixel ratio capped at 2 (1 at LOW).
  Levels: each level has its own scene, camera, and unit scale, so the near to far ratio per level stays under 1e5 without a logarithmic depth buffer. A level manager loads, activates, and disposes levels. On exit it disposes every GPU resource the level owns: geometry, material, texture, render target.
  Texture cache: an LRU of baked planet targets (12 entries at 1024 per face; the focused planet may hold 2048). Evicted entries are disposed immediately.
  Context loss: handle webglcontextlost and webglcontextrestored; on restore, rebake what is visible.
  No WebGL2: show a static panel that says so.

QUALITY TIERS:
  LOW: bake 512, 60k galaxy points, rim atmosphere, no cloud shadows, bloom at half resolution, DPR 1.
  MEDIUM: bake 1024, 150k points, scattering 8/4, no cloud shadows.
  HIGH: bake 1024 with the focused planet at 2048, 300k points, scattering 16/8, cloud and ring shadows.
  ULTRA: bake 2048, 500k points, scattering 16/8, SMAA, close-up detail normal at full strength.
  Default: HIGH on desktop, MEDIUM on touch devices. Drop one tier automatically when the mean frame time stays above 20 ms for 3 seconds. The player can override the tier; persist the override in localStorage inside try/catch.

CAMERA AND INPUT:
  L3: arcball rotation with inertia and no polar clamp, so the poles are reachable. Wheel or pinch zoom, exponential, from 1.15 to 12 planet radii. The planet spins on its own tilted axis and the clouds spin independently.
  L2, L1, L0: orbit around the focus with a polar clamp of 5 to 175 degrees, damping 0.08, pan limited to the level bounds.
  Touch: one finger rotates, pinch zooms, two fingers pan.
  Keyboard: Escape goes up one level, [ and ] step to the previous and next planet, Space pauses time, F focuses the selection, R resets the view.
  Picking: raycast against meshes at L2 and L3. At L1 and L0 use a screen-space nearest-point search over projected positions in a uniform grid with a 12 px hover radius. Only selectable systems and galaxies are pickable.

LEVEL TRANSITIONS:
  Down (click a galaxy, a system, or a planet): the camera eases to the target over 900 to 1400 ms (ease in-out cubic) while the target grows. The next level starts with its camera placed so the target's on-screen size matches. Both levels render to targets and crossfade for about 300 ms. The camera never cuts.
  Moon at L3: clicking a moon moves the L3 focus to that moon with the same dive.
  Up (Escape, breadcrumb, or zooming past the level's maximum distance): the reverse of the dive, ending with the previous focus centered. Zoom-out triggers only after the wheel keeps pushing past the maximum for 250 ms, so the level does not flicker at the boundary.
  Input is locked during a transition, and Escape snaps to the end state.
  With prefers-reduced-motion the dive becomes a 200 ms fade.

 HUD (sci-fi RTS military console, original design):
  Top left: breadcrumb (Universe > galaxy > system > planet), each part clickable.
  Top right: seed bar with seed input, randomize, and copy deep link.
  Right: info panel for the hovered or selected object: name, type, class, radius, mass, gravity, temperature, orbit period, day length, moons, atmosphere.
  L2 and L3: system roster with live thumbnails of every planet (small offscreen renders); clicking one flies there.
  L2: time controls at the bottom.
  3D labels: HTML overlay positioned per frame, overlap-culled, at most 60 visible.
  Selection reticle: four corner brackets that snap in over 120 ms on hover and pulse slowly on the selected object.
  Visual language: near-black base, translucent steel-teal panels, 1 px hairlines, chamfered 8 px corners via clip-path, small-caps section labels with 0.08 em tracking, tabular numerals, one cyan accent for selection and one amber accent for warnings.
  Below 720 px width the side panels collapse into a bottom sheet.
  Debug overlay (?debug=1 or F3): fps, frame ms, draw calls, renderer.info.memory counts, bake queue, current ID; toggles per layer (clouds, atmosphere, rings, bloom); a planet lab that forces any planet type with a seed slider; a flat preview of the six baked cube faces.

